Notes for the writer, not part of the description: the description starts at the `## What to build` line below the
rule, and nothing above the rule is written to Leeway.

`plan-project` writes every task description in this shape. Every heading is filled: "nothing unbounded here" is an
answer, a missing Bounds is not. A description holds at most 5,000 characters, and Leeway shows raw HTML as text,
so the description carries no HTML comment or other marker.

---

## What to build
The types, columns, endpoints and constraints. Name files.

## Serves
The criteria this task delivers, acceptance and technical, by number and story —
`AC-2, TC-1 (<story id>)` — and the hazards it owns. A task that can name neither is scope drift or a criterion the story forgot.

## Bounds
What unbounded thing does this touch — a connection, a result set, a parse over remote output, a
pool? Name the ceiling and who enforces it, or write "nothing unbounded here" and mean it.

## Parity
Which sibling already does this, and what must match it. Name the file. Deviating is allowed;
deviating without noticing is what produces behaviour nobody asked for.
When the sibling does the same job as the new code, not just looks like it, write **extract** (a
shared component or function, both call sites move to it) or **copy**, and why. Decided at the plan
gate, by a human when it changes the scope.

## Negative case
What must be proven NOT to happen. A caller that must see nothing, an input that must be refused,
a state that must not be reachable.

## Evidence
The rung, and the specific proof it owes.

## Out of scope
What this task must not touch, especially files another task in this story owns.

## Done when
Outcomes, at least one of which is something PROVEN FALSE — not only things that pass.
